  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math> of an ideal gas is allowed to expand isothermally into vacuum until the total volume is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math>. The amount of heat absorbed in this expansion is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></math> atm.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "0",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In case of expansion, work is done by system.</p><p>Now, as we know that,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mrow><mi>ext</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p><p>As the gas is expanding into&nbsp;vacuums which has no pressure, i.e.,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mrow><mi>ext</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p>Hence, no work is done.</p><p>From first law of thermodynamics,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#916;U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math></p><p>As the system is working at constant temperature, i.e., isothermally.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mi>&#916;U</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p>Hence&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>",
